 "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2025-12-09T14:40:55Z", "username": "Ryan Colaço", "text": "X = Consistent word spacing Y = Writing / script Writing long predates medieval Europe, but routine, consistent word spacing is relatively late. Insular manuscripts (Ireland & Britain, 7th–9th c.) are where systematic spacing first appears. Classical Greek & Latin used _scriptio continua_, with spacing only used sporadically for novices. Scholars fluent in the language didn’t need spacing; Irish monks learning Latin did, so they innovated. Spacing later proved to speed silent reading and reduce copying errors, spreading across Europe.", "role": "answer_reveal", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2025-12-09T14:42:28Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Bhai brilliant question", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}]